## Files

- `run_local/`, `run_ibex/` — raw output of `code/run_cv.py`, one directory per machine:
  - `folds.csv`: one row per outer fold (50). `repeat` 0-9, `fold` 0-4, `outer_fold` 0-49,
    `n_train` 120, `n_test` 30, `acc_lr`, `acc_knn` (multiples of 1/30), `diff_lr_minus_knn`,
    `correct_lr`, `correct_knn` (out of 30), `k_selected`, `k_inner_acc` (best inner-CV accuracy),
    `lr_n_iter` (lbfgs iterations).
  - `predictions.csv`: one row per test case per fold (1500): `test_index` (row of the ARFF, 0-based),
    `y_true`, `pred_lr`, `pred_knn` (0 setosa, 1 versicolor, 2 virginica), `prob_lr_0..2`.
  - `inner_scores.csv`: inner-CV mean and sd accuracy of every k on every outer fold (50 x 16).
  - `run_info.json`: host, SLURM ids, CPU model, Python and package versions, BLAS
    (threadpoolctl), thread settings, data sha256, sha256 of the run files, seeds, k grid, wall time.
- `folds_long.csv` — the local `folds.csv` in long form for plotting: `repeat, fold, method`
  (`LR`/`kNN`), `accuracy, correct, n_test, k_selected` (kNN rows only).
- `summary.json` (local, primary) and `summary_ibex.json` — output of `code/summarize.py`.
  Also `fold_sd` and `repetition_means` (10 values) per quantity.
- `identity.json` — output of `code/compare_runs.py`.
- `numbers.tex` — output of `code/make_numbers.py`; accuracies in percent, differences in
  percentage points, one decimal. `\DataURL` holds a URL: use `\expandafter\url\expandafter{\DataURL}`.

## Interval method

Primary, for each method's mean accuracy and for the mean paired difference: the corrected
resampled t interval (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; "corrected repeated k-fold CV test", Bouckaert &
Frank 2004). With J = 50 fold values x_j, mean m and sample variance s^2,
m +- t(0.975, 49) * sqrt((1/50 + 30/120) * s^2). The unit is the outer fold. The correction
widens the naive interval about 3.7-fold, because the training sets of different folds overlap.
It is still an approximation; coverage can fall short of nominal (Bates, Hastie & Tibshirani 2023).
The naive t over folds and the t over repetition means are reported only as labelled
comparisons. Both ignore the dependence between folds and are much narrower.
Bounds are not clipped to [0, 1].

## Identity of the local and Ibex runs (plan D6)

Both runs used the same `run_cv.py`, lock file, launchers and data file. `identity.json` checks
this: `same_code` compares the run files' sha256 and `same_data` compares the data sha256. Both
runs are single-threaded. The primary replicate is Ibex job 53905964 on node cn504-20 (AMD EPYC 9655).
It is **identical** to the local run (Intel Xeon Gold 6230) at L1 (all 1500 predicted labels per
method, the selected k and the fold accuracies) and at L2 (all printed result macros). It is also
identical in the diagnostics: all three output CSVs are byte-identical, LR probabilities included.
On both machines OpenBLAS selects its SkylakeX kernels (`info_*.blas`).

Recorded side result: `ibex_other_node/` holds an earlier replicate of the same design.
It is Ibex job 53905853 on node cn113-35-l (AMD EPYC 7702), where OpenBLAS selects its Haswell kernels.
Its `run_cv.py` differed from the final one only in which files it hashes into `run_info.json`
(so `same_code` is false there); the computation was the same. It is also identical at L1 and L2,
but its LR predicted probabilities differ from the local run by up to 4.9e-14
(`ibex_other_node/identity.json` -> `diagnostics`). So on this hardware pairing, bitwise identity
of intermediate floating-point values depends on the CPU's BLAS kernel. Every reported number
(accuracies, k, intervals) is identical on all three machines.
